      "side": 0,
      "message": "Callers can either use `spi_transaction_async()` followed by `spi_transaction_flush()`, or they can use the blocking `spi_transaction()`, which is just a wrapper around the two calls.\n\nFor write-then-read transactions, `spi_transaction_start()` does first `spi_dma_start()` to initiate the write part, then `spi_dma_wait()` before invoking `spi_dma_start()` to initiate the read part, and then returns while the DMA read is  ongoing.  Later, `spi_transaction_flush()` will invoke `spi_dma_wait()` a second time to wait for the read part to complete.  This works as intended, as the write part will typically be one a few bytes, and we have to tolerate blocking while those are transmitted.\n\nFor write-only transactions, however, before my change `spi_transaction_start()` does first `spi_dma_start()` to initiate the write part, and then `spi_dma_wait()` in line 386 waiting for the entire transaction to complete, before retuning.  Later, when `spi_transaction_flush()` is called, it will call `spi_dma_wait()` a second time, despite the dma having been confirmed finished already.  I find that counter-intuitive, and refuse to believe it to have been the intention.\n\nTo answer your question directly.  For write-only transactions we do NOT need or want to wait for the TX inside this function that initiates a transfer.",
